1
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:09,738
Imagine standing in a place so cold that your breath freezes before it hits the

2
00:00:09,778 --> 00:00:10,400
ground.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,305
Where the wind can strip the heat from your body in minutes.

4
00:00:15,345 --> 00:00:18,378
Where every direction looks exactly the same.

5
00:00:19,478 --> 00:00:20,937
No roads. No trees.

6
00:00:20,977 --> 00:00:25,230
No sound except the ice, groaning beneath your feet.

7
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:30,575
And the nearest human being... is thirty kilometers away.

8
00:00:31,675 --> 00:00:33,310
This is the story of Noah.

9
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:37,238
And of the twelve days that taught him what really happens...

10
00:00:37,278 --> 00:00:39,747
when you are stranded in the Arctic.

11
00:00:49,347 --> 00:00:53,007
It was supposed to be the adventure of a lifetime.

12
00:00:54,107 --> 00:00:58,557
Noah had been hired for three weeks as a technician at Nordhavn,

13
00:00:58,597 --> 00:01:02,555
a small research station on the edge of the Arctic Ocean.

14
00:01:02,595 --> 00:01:08,097
His job was simple: install and test new weather sensors before the long winter

15
00:01:08,137 --> 00:01:09,269
night arrived.

16
00:01:10,369 --> 00:01:15,947
The last leg of the journey was a four-hour flight in a small propeller plane,

17
00:01:15,987 --> 00:01:20,962
loaded with supplies, fuel drums and boxes of scientific equipment.

18
00:01:22,062 --> 00:01:24,757
He had taken the job almost by accident.

19
00:01:24,797 --> 00:01:28,586
A friend of a friend had dropped out at the last minute,

20
00:01:28,626 --> 00:01:32,757
and Noah had said yes before he could talk himself out of it.

21
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:36,571
He wanted, just once, to go somewhere truly far away.

22
00:01:37,671 --> 00:01:42,383
The pilot was a woman named Ingrid Halvorsen. Fifty-six years old.

23
00:01:42,423 --> 00:01:46,774
Thirty years flying in the north. She had landed on glaciers,

24
00:01:46,814 --> 00:01:52,508
frozen lakes and stretches of sea ice that most pilots wouldn't even look at.

25
00:01:53,608 --> 00:01:55,466
First time this far north?

26
00:01:56,566 --> 00:01:57,763
That obvious?

27
00:01:58,863 --> 00:02:03,203
You've been staring out the window for two hours like a child at an aquarium.

28
00:02:03,243 --> 00:02:03,886
Don't worry.

29
00:02:03,926 --> 00:02:05,782
Everyone does, the first time.

30
00:02:06,882 --> 00:02:09,787
People think the Arctic is empty. It isn't. It's full.

31
00:02:09,827 --> 00:02:14,315
Full of wind, and light, and things that have learned to live where nothing should.

32
00:02:14,355 --> 00:02:16,359
You just have to know how to look.

33
00:02:17,459 --> 00:02:20,567
Below them, the world was white and endless.

34
00:02:20,607 --> 00:02:26,624
Mountains, then glaciers, then the frozen sea, cracked into a giant puzzle of ice.

35
00:02:27,724 --> 00:02:30,967
It was the most beautiful thing Noah had ever seen.

36
00:02:31,007 --> 00:02:36,372
He took a photo with his phone, and sent it to his sister. It never went through.

37
00:02:37,472 --> 00:02:42,680
An hour from Nordhavn, Ingrid tapped the fuel gauge... and stopped smiling.

38
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:50,802
A storm front had moved in from the north.

39
00:02:50,842 --> 00:02:53,646
Faster than any forecast had predicted.

40
00:02:53,686 --> 00:02:56,461
In minutes, the horizon disappeared.

41
00:02:57,561 --> 00:03:00,850
Hold on to something. This is going to get bumpy.

42
00:03:01,950 --> 00:03:05,568
The plane dropped and lurched like a leaf in the wind.

43
00:03:05,608 --> 00:03:07,533
Snow hammered the windshield.

44
00:03:07,573 --> 00:03:09,958
The instruments began to flicker.

45
00:03:11,058 --> 00:03:13,070
Ice was forming on the wings.

46
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:15,808
The engine coughed once, then again.

47
00:03:16,908 --> 00:03:18,398
Mayday, mayday, mayday.

48
00:03:18,438 --> 00:03:23,054
Charlie Kilo Four, losing altitude, sixty miles southwest of Nordhavn.

49
00:03:23,094 --> 00:03:25,240
Attempting landing on sea ice.

50
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:29,296
There was no answer. Only static.

51
00:03:30,396 --> 00:03:35,494
Ingrid searched the white chaos below for anything flat. Anything solid.

52
00:03:36,594 --> 00:03:38,339
Brace! Brace! Brace!

53
00:03:45,439 --> 00:03:50,646
When Noah opened his eyes, the world was sideways. The engine was silent.

54
00:03:50,686 --> 00:03:53,136
And the only sound was the wind.

55
00:03:54,236 --> 00:03:59,884
The plane had skidded three hundred meters across the ice before a ridge of frozen

56
00:03:59,924 --> 00:04:05,277
snow tore off its left wing. But the cabin was intact. And they were alive.

57
00:04:10,377 --> 00:04:14,484
Here is something most people don't know about extreme cold.

58
00:04:14,524 --> 00:04:19,996
It doesn't kill you dramatically. It kills you quietly. One degree at a time.

59
00:04:21,096 --> 00:04:25,519
At minus forty, exposed skin can freeze in less than ten minutes.

60
00:04:25,559 --> 00:04:30,599
Your body pulls blood away from your hands and feet to protect your heart.

61
00:04:30,639 --> 00:04:32,384
Your fingers stop working.

62
00:04:32,424 --> 00:04:34,565
Then your thoughts slow down.

63
00:04:35,665 --> 00:04:40,269
The most dangerous moment is often the first hour after an accident,

64
00:04:40,309 --> 00:04:44,146
when shock and adrenaline make people forget the cold.

65
00:04:45,246 --> 00:04:47,276
And then there is hypothermia.

66
00:04:47,316 --> 00:04:50,727
When your core temperature drops just two degrees,

67
00:04:50,767 --> 00:04:53,142
you start to shiver uncontrollably.

68
00:04:53,182 --> 00:04:56,455
At four degrees, you become confused and clumsy.

69
00:04:56,495 --> 00:04:58,801
Below that, the shivering stops...

70
00:04:58,841 --> 00:05:02,028
and that is when it becomes truly dangerous.

71
00:05:03,128 --> 00:05:09,345
Noah's first instinct was to run outside and look for help. Ingrid grabbed his arm.

72
00:05:10,445 --> 00:05:11,356
No. Listen to me.

73
00:05:11,396 --> 00:05:15,605
Nobody walks anywhere in this. We stay with the plane. The plane is shelter.

74
00:05:15,645 --> 00:05:18,088
The plane is what they'll be looking for.

75
00:05:19,188 --> 00:05:23,366
They blocked the broken window with seat cushions and a tarp.

76
00:05:23,406 --> 00:05:25,648
They put on every layer they had.

77
00:05:25,688 --> 00:05:31,577
Noah found a small camping stove in the emergency kit, and a box of fuel canisters.

78
00:05:32,677 --> 00:05:37,241
The first blue flame was the most beautiful thing he had ever seen.

79
00:05:37,281 --> 00:05:40,729
More beautiful, even, than the ice from the sky.

80
00:05:41,829 --> 00:05:44,787
Outside, the storm raged for thirty hours.

81
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:48,141
Inside, two strangers sat shoulder to shoulder,

82
00:05:48,181 --> 00:05:51,900
listening to the wind try to tear the plane apart.

83
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:53,503
Day one.

84
00:05:53,543 --> 00:05:55,473
I'm recording this in case...

85
00:05:55,513 --> 00:05:59,343
in case someone finds the phone. We crashed. We're alive.

86
00:05:59,383 --> 00:06:01,177
The pilot's name is Ingrid.

87
00:06:01,217 --> 00:06:03,825
She's the calmest person I've ever met.

88
00:06:03,865 --> 00:06:07,139
I'm trying very hard to be the second calmest.

89
00:06:12,239 --> 00:06:17,033
When the storm finally passed, Noah saw how badly Ingrid was hurt.

90
00:06:18,133 --> 00:06:20,577
Her left leg was broken below the knee.

91
00:06:20,617 --> 00:06:23,443
She had splinted it herself during the night,

92
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:28,729
with a piece of the instrument panel and a roll of tape, and hadn't said a word.

93
00:06:29,829 --> 00:06:31,232
Why didn't you tell me?

94
00:06:32,332 --> 00:06:36,558
Because you would have panicked. And I needed you calm. You did well, by the way.

95
00:06:37,658 --> 00:06:38,674
They took stock.

96
00:06:38,714 --> 00:06:41,445
The radio had been destroyed in the crash.

97
00:06:41,485 --> 00:06:44,084
The emergency beacon was transmitting...

98
00:06:44,124 --> 00:06:46,196
but its antenna had snapped off,

99
00:06:46,236 --> 00:06:49,817
and nobody knew how far the weak signal could reach.

100
00:06:50,917 --> 00:06:54,656
They had food for maybe ten days, if they were careful.

101
00:06:54,696 --> 00:06:58,505
Fuel for the stove, about the same. And snow, of course.

102
00:06:58,545 --> 00:07:01,306
Endless snow, that could become water.

103
00:07:02,406 --> 00:07:08,107
Noah learned his first rule of the Arctic from Ingrid that morning. Never eat snow.

104
00:07:08,147 --> 00:07:09,559
Always melt it first.

105
00:07:09,599 --> 00:07:14,729
Eating snow lowers your body temperature, and in the cold, heat is life.

106
00:07:15,829 --> 00:07:19,636
Out here, everything is about energy. Every movement costs heat.

107
00:07:19,676 --> 00:07:24,034
Every mistake costs heat. You don't waste anything. Not even a shiver.

108
00:07:25,134 --> 00:07:26,239
Tell me about home.

109
00:07:26,279 --> 00:07:29,502
Talking keeps the mind warm. Who's waiting for you?

110
00:07:30,602 --> 00:07:31,245
My sister.

111
00:07:31,285 --> 00:07:33,774
She's the one who always calls first.

112
00:07:33,814 --> 00:07:36,645
She told me this trip was a terrible idea.

113
00:07:36,685 --> 00:07:39,022
I told her she worries too much.

114
00:07:40,122 --> 00:07:43,541
Then we'll have to get you home, so you can tell her she was right.

115
00:07:44,641 --> 00:07:48,382
She taught him how to build a snow wall to block the wind.

116
00:07:48,422 --> 00:07:53,141
How to check his own face and fingers for the white patches of frostbite.

117
00:07:53,181 --> 00:07:58,742
How to sleep with his boots inside his sleeping bag, so they wouldn't freeze solid.

118
00:07:59,842 --> 00:08:02,766
And she told him stories. About the north.

119
00:08:02,806 --> 00:08:07,140
About the people who had survived here for thousands of years,

120
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:09,447
long before planes and radios.

121
00:08:14,547 --> 00:08:15,866
It was late October.

122
00:08:15,906 --> 00:08:20,609
In this part of the world, the sun was disappearing for the winter.

123
00:08:21,709 --> 00:08:25,698
Each day, it rose a little lower, and set a little sooner.

124
00:08:25,738 --> 00:08:29,589
On the fourth day, it barely cleared the horizon at all,

125
00:08:29,629 --> 00:08:34,225
a pale orange disc sliding sideways along the edge of the world.

126
00:08:35,325 --> 00:08:37,496
Soon there would be no sun at all.

127
00:08:37,536 --> 00:08:42,368
Only months of darkness, and the light of the moon and stars on the ice.

128
00:08:43,468 --> 00:08:45,955
Day four. No planes. No helicopters.

129
00:08:45,995 --> 00:08:47,991
I keep listening for engines.

130
00:08:48,031 --> 00:08:53,376
Every gust of wind sounds like rescue for a second... and then it doesn't.

131
00:08:54,476 --> 00:08:57,532
Every morning, they performed the same ritual.

132
00:08:57,572 --> 00:09:03,052
Ingrid checked Noah's nose, cheeks and ears for the waxy white spots of frostbite.

133
00:09:03,092 --> 00:09:05,542
Noah checked her fingers, one by one.

134
00:09:05,582 --> 00:09:10,511
It became a kind of greeting. Good morning. You're still here. So am I.

135
00:09:11,611 --> 00:09:14,512
On the fifth night, the sky caught fire.

136
00:09:15,612 --> 00:09:20,682
Curtains of green light rippled across the darkness, folding and unfolding,

137
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:23,870
so bright that the snow glowed beneath them.

138
00:09:24,970 --> 00:09:28,528
My grandmother used to say you should never whistle at the northern lights.

139
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:30,925
Or they will come down and take you with them.

140
00:09:32,025 --> 00:09:33,324
Do you believe that?

141
00:09:34,424 --> 00:09:37,105
I believe it's not a good night to find out.

142
00:09:38,205 --> 00:09:41,357
That night, Ingrid told him why she still flew,

143
00:09:41,397 --> 00:09:44,006
at an age when most pilots had retired.

144
00:09:44,046 --> 00:09:46,248
Her husband had been a pilot too.

145
00:09:46,288 --> 00:09:50,663
Twenty years ago, he had gone down in a storm, not far from here.

146
00:09:50,703 --> 00:09:52,687
They never found the plane.

147
00:09:53,787 --> 00:09:55,718
Everyone told me to stop flying.

148
00:09:55,758 --> 00:09:59,660
But up here, I always feel like he's just over the next horizon.

149
00:09:59,700 --> 00:10:02,068
So I keep going to the next horizon.

150
00:10:03,168 --> 00:10:06,785
For the first time since the crash, they both laughed.

151
00:10:06,825 --> 00:10:12,596
And for a few minutes, under that impossible sky, the cold didn't seem so terrible.

152
00:10:17,696 --> 00:10:21,910
On the sixth day, Noah woke to a sound he would never forget.

153
00:10:21,950 --> 00:10:25,727
A deep, heavy crack, like a cannon fired underwater.

154
00:10:26,827 --> 00:10:28,987
Sea ice is not land. It floats.

155
00:10:29,027 --> 00:10:33,932
It drifts with the wind and the currents. And sometimes, it breaks.

156
00:10:35,032 --> 00:10:40,013
Two hundred meters from the plane, a black line had opened in the white.

157
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:43,829
A channel of open water, steaming in the frozen air.

158
00:10:44,929 --> 00:10:46,272
The pack is breaking up.

159
00:10:46,312 --> 00:10:50,017
If that crack keeps growing, we could end up on an island of ice.

160
00:10:50,057 --> 00:10:51,359
Drifting out to sea.

161
00:10:52,459 --> 00:10:57,091
Ingrid checked her compass against the stars. The ice had already moved.

162
00:10:57,131 --> 00:11:00,012
They were drifting east, away from the coast.

163
00:11:00,052 --> 00:11:03,057
Away from where any search would be looking.

164
00:11:04,157 --> 00:11:06,217
That night, neither of them slept.

165
00:11:06,257 --> 00:11:08,688
They lay in the dark, feeling the faint,

166
00:11:08,728 --> 00:11:11,782
sickening sway of the whole world beneath them.

167
00:11:12,882 --> 00:11:17,605
The next morning, they moved everything they could to the side of the plane

168
00:11:17,644 --> 00:11:20,843
farthest from the crack. Food, fuel, sleeping bags.

169
00:11:20,883 --> 00:11:23,383
It wouldn't save them if the floe broke.

170
00:11:23,423 --> 00:11:25,986
But it felt better than doing nothing.

171
00:11:31,086 --> 00:11:33,879
On the seventh day, they had a visitor.

172
00:11:34,979 --> 00:11:38,129
Noah saw it first, through the cracked window.

173
00:11:38,169 --> 00:11:41,250
A shape moving across the ice. Slow. Patient.

174
00:11:41,290 --> 00:11:43,868
Yellow-white against the blue snow.

175
00:11:44,968 --> 00:11:45,846
A polar bear.

176
00:11:45,886 --> 00:11:48,318
The largest land predator on Earth.

177
00:11:48,358 --> 00:11:51,827
It had smelled their food from kilometers away.

178
00:11:52,927 --> 00:11:54,715
Don't move. Don't make a sound.

179
00:11:54,755 --> 00:11:57,204
Get the flare gun from the kit. Slowly.

180
00:11:58,304 --> 00:12:01,111
The bear circled the plane once. Twice.

181
00:12:01,151 --> 00:12:06,076
It stood on its hind legs and pressed its nose against the fuselage,

182
00:12:06,116 --> 00:12:11,668
just a few centimeters from Noah's face, separated by a sheet of aluminum.

183
00:12:15,768 --> 00:12:18,498
Ingrid nodded. Noah pushed open the door,

184
00:12:18,538 --> 00:12:23,621
aimed the flare gun into the snow between them and the bear... and fired.

185
00:12:24,721 --> 00:12:28,459
A burst of red light and smoke exploded across the ice.

186
00:12:28,499 --> 00:12:30,383
The bear flinched, turned...

187
00:12:30,423 --> 00:12:35,999
and loped away into the twilight, unhurried, as if it had simply lost interest.

188
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,460
I think my heart stopped.

189
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:40,219
I think it actually stopped.

190
00:12:41,319 --> 00:12:43,422
It didn't. I can hear it from here.

191
00:12:44,522 --> 00:12:46,246
But they both knew the truth.

192
00:12:46,286 --> 00:12:50,572
They had used one of their three flares. And the bear would be back.

193
00:12:55,672 --> 00:13:00,226
On the eighth day, Ingrid spread her old paper chart across her knees,

194
00:13:00,266 --> 00:13:03,173
and pointed to a tiny square on the coast.

195
00:13:04,273 --> 00:13:06,418
Kapp Varde. An old weather station.

196
00:13:06,458 --> 00:13:11,413
Unmanned, but they still keep a radio there for emergencies. Fifteen kilometers.

197
00:13:11,453 --> 00:13:12,852
Maybe a little more.

198
00:13:13,952 --> 00:13:17,973
With her broken leg, Ingrid couldn't walk a hundred meters.

199
00:13:18,013 --> 00:13:23,188
If anyone was going to reach that radio, it would have to be Noah. Alone.

200
00:13:24,288 --> 00:13:29,316
I'm not leaving you here alone. With that bear out there. With the ice breaking up.

201
00:13:30,416 --> 00:13:31,903
Noah. The ice is drifting.

202
00:13:31,943 --> 00:13:34,253
Every day, we're farther from the coast.

203
00:13:34,293 --> 00:13:37,131
In three days, that station will be out of reach.

204
00:13:37,171 --> 00:13:39,024
Then neither of us goes home.

205
00:13:40,124 --> 00:13:44,841
She gave him the compass, two of the remaining flares, half of the food,

206
00:13:44,881 --> 00:13:49,987
and a set of instructions he repeated until he could say them in his sleep.

207
00:13:51,087 --> 00:13:53,233
Walk with the wind on your left cheek.

208
00:13:53,273 --> 00:13:56,858
Rest every hour, but never sit down for more than five minutes.

209
00:13:56,898 --> 00:13:59,102
If you start to feel warm and sleepy...

210
00:13:59,142 --> 00:14:01,766
that is the cold lying to you. Keep moving.

211
00:14:02,866 --> 00:14:07,441
At the first gray light of the ninth day, Noah stepped out onto the ice,

212
00:14:07,481 --> 00:14:09,298
and started walking north.

213
00:14:10,398 --> 00:14:11,744
He looked back once.

214
00:14:11,784 --> 00:14:14,584
A tiny broken plane in an ocean of white.

215
00:14:14,624 --> 00:14:17,199
And then he didn't look back again.

216
00:14:22,299 --> 00:14:27,939
The first five kilometers were almost easy. The air was still. The snow was firm.

217
00:14:27,979 --> 00:14:31,025
Noah counted his steps, a hundred at a time,

218
00:14:31,065 --> 00:14:34,300
and checked the compass after every hundred.

219
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:37,566
Then the wind came back.

220
00:14:38,666 --> 00:14:42,273
It came low across the ice, carrying snow like sand.

221
00:14:42,313 --> 00:14:46,480
Within an hour, he could barely see his own boots. Whiteout.

222
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:51,088
The sky and the ground melted into one blank, featureless glow.

223
00:14:52,188 --> 00:14:56,131
In a whiteout, people walk in circles without realizing it.

224
00:14:56,171 --> 00:14:58,696
Their brain has nothing to hold on to.

225
00:14:58,736 --> 00:15:03,814
Noah kept his eyes on the compass needle, and the wind on his left cheek.

226
00:15:04,914 --> 00:15:09,185
One hundred. One hundred steps. Check. Okay. Again. One. Two. Three...

227
00:15:10,285 --> 00:15:15,454
He crossed pressure ridges, walls of broken ice pushed up like frozen waves.

228
00:15:15,494 --> 00:15:20,921
He jumped across narrow cracks, and walked long detours around the wide ones.

229
00:15:22,021 --> 00:15:25,930
Somewhere in the fourth hour, he realized he wasn't alone.

230
00:15:25,970 --> 00:15:30,695
A small white shape was trotting along beside him, thirty meters away.

231
00:15:30,735 --> 00:15:31,648
An Arctic fox.

232
00:15:31,688 --> 00:15:37,489
It followed him for almost an hour, stopping when he stopped, moving when he moved.

233
00:15:38,589 --> 00:15:40,471
Are you here to keep me company?

234
00:15:40,511 --> 00:15:44,805
Or waiting to see if I fall down? Either way... I'm glad you're here.

235
00:15:45,905 --> 00:15:50,614
Then, in the seventh hour, the ice gave way beneath his right foot.

236
00:15:51,714 --> 00:15:56,719
He threw himself forward and rolled, and the water only reached his knee.

237
00:15:56,759 --> 00:16:00,243
But at minus forty, a wet foot is a death sentence.

238
00:16:00,283 --> 00:16:04,787
In minutes, the boot would freeze solid, and so would his toes.

239
00:16:05,887 --> 00:16:07,731
He remembered Ingrid's voice.

240
00:16:07,771 --> 00:16:12,277
He rolled in the dry snow to soak up the water before it could freeze.

241
00:16:12,317 --> 00:16:13,771
He pulled off the boot,

242
00:16:13,811 --> 00:16:18,318
changed into the spare socks she had made him carry inside his jacket,

243
00:16:18,358 --> 00:16:19,422
and kept walking.

244
00:16:19,462 --> 00:16:23,574
The whole thing took three minutes. It felt like three hours.

245
00:16:24,674 --> 00:16:28,041
By the sixth hour, he could no longer feel his toes.

246
00:16:28,081 --> 00:16:31,251
By the eighth, his thoughts had started to drift.

247
00:16:31,291 --> 00:16:35,641
He found himself thinking about summer. About his sister's kitchen.

248
00:16:35,681 --> 00:16:38,976
About lying down in the snow, just for a minute.

249
00:16:40,076 --> 00:16:42,738
If you start to feel warm and sleepy...

250
00:16:42,778 --> 00:16:45,006
that is the cold lying to you.

251
00:16:46,106 --> 00:16:49,725
Keep moving. Keep moving, Noah. She's waiting. Keep moving.

252
00:16:50,825 --> 00:16:52,855
Night fell. The wind dropped.

253
00:16:52,895 --> 00:16:58,707
And above him, once again, the northern lights began to dance. Green. Then violet.

254
00:16:58,747 --> 00:17:00,110
Then green again.

255
00:17:01,210 --> 00:17:05,554
And there, beneath them, on a low hill by the frozen shore...

256
00:17:05,594 --> 00:17:09,410
a thin black line pointed at the sky. A radio mast.

257
00:17:14,510 --> 00:17:19,707
Kapp Varde weather station was three small huts, half buried in snowdrifts,

258
00:17:19,747 --> 00:17:21,713
and a mast covered in ice.

259
00:17:22,813 --> 00:17:24,527
The door was frozen shut.

260
00:17:24,567 --> 00:17:30,541
Noah broke the ice from the handle with the butt of the flare gun, and fell inside.

261
00:17:31,641 --> 00:17:35,586
It was barely warmer than outside. But there was no wind.

262
00:17:35,626 --> 00:17:41,439
And on a shelf, under a layer of frost, sat a satellite radio and a battery pack.

263
00:17:42,539 --> 00:17:47,303
His hands were so numb he had to press the buttons with his knuckles.

264
00:17:48,403 --> 00:17:53,296
Mayday. Mayday. This is... this is Noah. From the plane. Charlie Kilo Four.

265
00:17:53,336 --> 00:17:55,327
Is anyone receiving? Please.

266
00:17:59,427 --> 00:18:04,008
Charlie Kilo Four, this is Nordhavn station. We read you. Say again, we read you!

267
00:18:04,048 --> 00:18:05,111
Are you injured?

268
00:18:06,211 --> 00:18:08,199
Noah couldn't answer at first.

269
00:18:08,239 --> 00:18:11,836
He just held the radio against his chest and cried.

270
00:18:12,936 --> 00:18:15,883
Then the engineer in him took over again.

271
00:18:15,923 --> 00:18:19,307
He read the coordinates from the station's GPS.

272
00:18:19,347 --> 00:18:22,703
He described the drift, the crack, the wing.

273
00:18:23,803 --> 00:18:25,428
Copy all. Noah, listen to me.

274
00:18:25,468 --> 00:18:27,668
We called off the air search yesterday.

275
00:18:27,708 --> 00:18:30,251
Everyone thought you went down in open water.

276
00:18:30,291 --> 00:18:32,164
A helicopter is launching now.

277
00:18:33,264 --> 00:18:34,704
They had stopped looking.

278
00:18:34,744 --> 00:18:38,329
If he had stayed with the plane... no one would have come.

279
00:18:39,429 --> 00:18:41,688
While he waited for the helicopter,

280
00:18:41,728 --> 00:18:46,679
Noah wrapped himself in every blanket he could find and stared at the radio.

281
00:18:46,719 --> 00:18:51,794
Every few minutes he pressed the button, just to hear a human voice answer.

282
00:18:52,894 --> 00:18:55,280
And as the adrenaline drained away,

283
00:18:55,320 --> 00:19:00,618
Noah measured the distance on the station's wall map. Not fifteen kilometers.

284
00:19:00,658 --> 00:19:01,570
Thirty-one.

285
00:19:02,670 --> 00:19:03,701
Ingrid had known.

286
00:19:03,741 --> 00:19:06,661
She had flown over this coast for thirty years.

287
00:19:06,701 --> 00:19:08,865
She had cut the distance in half...

288
00:19:08,905 --> 00:19:13,589
because she knew that if she told him the truth, he might not have gone.

289
00:19:18,689 --> 00:19:22,538
The helicopter arrived at Kapp Varde two hours later.

290
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:25,297
Noah refused to go to the hospital.

291
00:19:26,397 --> 00:19:29,177
She's out there. I know where. I'm coming with you.

292
00:19:30,277 --> 00:19:34,559
Nordhavn, Rescue One. Heading for coordinates. Thermal camera on. Two minutes out.

293
00:19:35,659 --> 00:19:40,020
They flew low over the ice, following the black line of the crack.

294
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:42,277
It had grown wider. Much wider.

295
00:19:43,377 --> 00:19:48,737
And there, on a floating island of ice barely bigger than a football field...

296
00:19:48,777 --> 00:19:49,719
was the plane.

297
00:19:49,759 --> 00:19:54,327
And beside it, a small red light. A flare. Burning in the snow.

298
00:19:55,427 --> 00:19:57,898
Ingrid had heard the helicopter coming.

299
00:19:57,938 --> 00:20:01,826
She had dragged herself out of the cabin with her broken leg,

300
00:20:01,866 --> 00:20:03,626
and fired her last flare.

301
00:20:04,726 --> 00:20:06,182
Took you long enough.

302
00:20:07,282 --> 00:20:09,198
You said fifteen kilometers.

303
00:20:10,298 --> 00:20:12,104
I said maybe a little more.

304
00:20:13,204 --> 00:20:15,695
Ingrid spent three weeks in hospital.

305
00:20:15,735 --> 00:20:20,948
Noah lost the tips of two toes to frostbite, and gained a friend for life.

306
00:20:22,048 --> 00:20:26,208
On her last day in hospital, Ingrid asked him a question.

307
00:20:27,308 --> 00:20:30,773
Next summer, I fly the coast again. To the next horizon.

308
00:20:30,813 --> 00:20:35,157
I need someone who knows how to walk thirty kilometers. Interested?

309
00:20:36,257 --> 00:20:40,540
When he finally called his sister from the hospital in Nordhavn,

310
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:45,741
the photo he had sent from the plane arrived on her phone at the same moment.

311
00:20:45,781 --> 00:20:49,173
A frozen sea, seen from above, eleven days late.

312
00:20:50,273 --> 00:20:52,147
Two weeks after the rescue,

313
00:20:52,187 --> 00:20:56,612
the ice floe that had carried the plane broke apart in a storm,

314
00:20:56,652 --> 00:20:58,999
and sank into the Arctic Ocean.

315
00:21:00,099 --> 00:21:03,844
When people ask Noah what he learned in those twelve days,

316
00:21:03,884 --> 00:21:07,754
he doesn't talk about the cold, or the bear, or the walk.

317
00:21:08,854 --> 00:21:13,866
He talks about a woman with a broken leg, who lied to him about the distance...

318
00:21:13,906 --> 00:21:17,445
because she believed he could make it, before he did.

319
00:21:18,545 --> 00:21:22,590
Because sometimes, survival isn't about being the strongest.

320
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:26,660
It's about having someone who refuses to let you give up.

321
00:21:32,760 --> 00:21:36,274
If you were in Noah's place, would you have made the walk?

322
00:21:36,314 --> 00:21:40,264
Or stayed with the plane, and waited? Tell us in the comments.

323
00:21:41,364 --> 00:21:42,408
And remember...

324
00:21:42,448 --> 00:21:45,416
when everything ends, the story begins.
